# NVIM POWER COURSE · OVERVIEW

Welcome. This course takes you from "I know the basics" to "the editor
disappears and I just think edits". It was generated for YOUR exact
setup — NvChad 2.5, your plugins, your custom mappings — so every key
shown here works on your machine.

If you have not finished the built-in basics yet, do these first:
`:Tutor vim-01-beginner`{vim} and `:Tutor vim-02-beginner`{vim}.

## HOW THIS WORKS

- Every chapter is a real buffer. Practice directly on its text.
- Shaded lines are exercises. The sign column shows ✗ until your edit
  produces the exact expected result, then it flips to ✓.
- These buffers cannot be written. Break everything fearlessly; running
  `:Tutor`{vim} again gives you a clean copy.
- Move the cursor onto a link and press `<Enter>`{normal} to follow it.
  Help links open the relevant `:help`{vim} page — read them, that is
  where the real depth lives.
- On a line that contains a command, type `>>`{normal} to run it. Try
  it on the line below:

    :echo "yes, >> works — you ran this from the tutor"

## THE CHAPTERS

Do them in order. One chapter per sitting is plenty. Repeat a chapter
until every exercise is muscle memory, not a puzzle.

1. [Precision motions](@tutor:power-01-motions) — put the cursor where
   the edit happens, in 1-2 keys
2. [Operators and text objects](@tutor:power-02-operators) — the
   grammar that makes vim vim
3. [Registers](@tutor:power-03-registers) — never lose a yank again
4. [Search, substitute, global](@tutor:power-04-search) — surgical
   bulk edits
5. [Macros](@tutor:power-05-macros) — record once, apply everywhere
6. [Marks, jumps, buffers, windows](@tutor:power-06-navigation) —
   fly around projects
7. [Visual block and number tricks](@tutor:power-07-visualblock) —
   column edits and auto-numbering
8. [IDE moves on your setup](@tutor:power-08-ide) — LSP, Telescope,
   gitsigns, terminals: everything VSCode did, but faster

## TRAINING RULES

1. No arrow keys. No mouse. If you catch yourself, undo and redo the
   move the vim way.
2. If you pressed the same motion three times in a row (jjj, wwww),
   stop — there was a better motion. Find it.
3. Think in sentences: verb + count + noun (`d`{normal} `2`{normal}
   `aw`{normal} = delete two words). Never "enter visual mode and
   wiggle" when a text object can say it directly.
4. After every change ask: could `.`{normal} repeat this? Edits that
   repeat with `.`{normal} are the fastest edits there are.
5. Turn on relative numbers while training: `:set rnu`{vim} (you have
   a toggle on `<leader>rn`{normal}). Counted jumps like `7j`{normal}
   become instant.

## DAILY DRILL (10 MINUTES)

- 2 min: reopen yesterday's chapter, redo its exercises at speed.
- 6 min: today's chapter.
- 2 min: in real code, consciously apply one new trick. One per day
  sticks; ten per day evaporate.

